an class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is the <span data-type="term" class="no-emphasis" id="term-00005" group-by="c">coefficient of kinetic friction</span>.</p>
</div></section></div>
<p id="fs-id1165038342161">A system in which <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is described as a system in which <em data-effect="italics">friction behaves simply</em>. The transition from static friction to kinetic friction is illustrated in <a href="6-2-friction#CNX_UPhysics_06_02_FrictGraph" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Figure 6.11">Figure 6.11</a>.</p>
<div class="os-figure" id="CNX_UPhysics_06_02_FrictGraph"><figure data-id="CNX_UPhysics_06_02_FrictGraph" class="ui-has-child-figcaption">
<span data-type="media" id="fs-id1165036793037" data-alt="(a) The figure shows a block on a horizontal surface. The situation is that of impending motion. The following forces are shown: N vertically up, w vertically down, F to the right, f sub s to the left. Vectors N and w are the same size. Vectors F and f sub s are the same size. (b) The figure shows a block on a horizontal surface. The motion is to the right. The situation is that of friction behaving simply. The following forces are shown: N vertically up, w vertically down, F to the right, f sub k to the left. Vectors N and w are the same size. Vectors F is larger than f sub s. (c) A graph of the magnitude of the friction force f as a function of the applied force F is shown. In the interval from 0 to when the magnitude of f equals f sub s max, the graph is a straight line described by f sub s equals F. This is the static region, and f sub s max equals mu sub s times N. For values of F larger than this maximum value of f, the graph drops a bit then flattens out to a somewhat noisy but constant on average value. This is the kinetic region in which the magnitude of f is f sub k which is also equal to mu sub k times N.">
<button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of (a) The figure shows a block on a horizontal surface. The situation is that of impending motion. The following forces are shown: N vertically up, w vertically down, F to the right, f sub s to the left. Vectors N and w are the same size. Vectors F and f sub s are the same size. (b) The figure shows a block on a horizontal surface. The motion is to the right. The situation is that of friction behaving simply. The following forces are shown: N vertically up, w vertically down, F to the right, f sub k to the left. Vectors N and w are the same size. Vectors F is larger than f sub s. (c) A graph of the magnitude of the friction force f as a function of the applied force F is shown. In the interval from 0 to when the magnitude of f equals f sub s max, the graph is a straight line described by f sub s equals F. This is the static region, and f sub s max equals mu sub s times N. For values of F larger than this maximum value of f, the graph drops a bit then flattens out to a somewhat noisy but constant on average value. This is the kinetic region in which the magnitude of f is f sub k which is also equal to mu sub k times N." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/ce2a5598ef05d7b31f11aef58c741615e6856bf0" data-media-type="image/jpeg" alt="(a) The figure shows a block on a horizontal surface. The situation is that of impending motion. The following forces are shown: N vertically up, w vertically down, F to the right, f sub s to the left. Vectors N and w are the same size. Vectors F and f sub s are the same size. (b) The figure shows a block on a horizontal surface. The motion is to the right. The situation is that of friction behaving simply. The following forces are shown: N vertically up, w vertically down, F to the right, f sub k to the left. Vectors N and w are the same size. Vectors F is larger than f sub s. (c) A graph of the magnitude of the friction force f as a function of the applied force F is shown. In the interval from 0 to when the magnitude of f equals f sub s max, the graph is a straight line described by f sub s equals F. This is the static region, and f sub s max equals mu sub s times N. For values of F larger than this maximum value of f, the graph drops a bit then flattens out to a somewhat noisy but constant on average value. This is the kinetic region in which the magnitude of f is f sub k which is also equal to mu sub k times N." width="974" height="417" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/ce2a5598ef05d7b31f11aef58c741615e6856bf0"></button>
</span>

<figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">6.11</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">(a) The force of friction <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle></annotation-xml></semantics></math> between the block and the rough surface opposes the direction of the applied force <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>.</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math> The magnitude of the static friction balances that of the applied force. This is shown in the left side of the graph in (c). (b) At some point, the magnitude of the applied force is greater than the force of kinetic friction, and the block moves to the right. This is shown in the right side of the graph. (c) The graph of the frictional force versus the applied force; note that <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math> This means that <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>k</mtext></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>
</span>
</figcaption></figure></div>
<p id="fs-id1165038339466">As you can see in <a href="6-2-friction#fs-id1165037150049" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Table 6.1">Table 6.1</a>, the coefficients of kinetic friction are less than their static counterparts. The approximate values of <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mi>μ</mi></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> are stated to only one or two digits to indicate the approximate description of friction given by the preceding two equations.</p>
<div class="os-table" id="fs-id1165037150049"><table data-id="fs-id1165037150049" data-summar